          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My father had a heart attack at 55. What does that mean for my own risk, and how does guidance use family history when deciding about a statin?",
          "answer": "**Safety first:** If you have chest pain, pressure, or tightness, shortness of breath, pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back, or sudden sweating or nausea, call emergency services now. Do not wait to see if it passes.\n\n**Your risk:** The retrieved material says a family history of premature coronary disease is one of several factors that contribute to heart disease. The others include smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, inactivity, and diet. Age and male sex also raise risk but cannot be changed. The rest can be modified, which is why a risk estimate can guide action (*Coronary artery disease*, General clinical reference, educational summary). The passages did not say how much a father's heart attack at 55 changes your personal risk, or what age counts as \"premature\" in guidance.\n\n**Statins and family history:** I could not find this in the retrieved material. The lipid screening and statin guideline was searched, but the passages returned covered other topics, such as blood pressure targets and heart failure. So I can't tell you how guidance weighs family history when deciding about a statin.\n\n**What to discuss with a clinician:** Ask whether your own cholesterol and blood pressure should be checked, and whether your overall risk estimate, including your father's history, calls for a statin discussion. Bring your father's age at his heart attack and any other relatives' heart history if you know it.\n\n*Sources: Coronary artery disease (General clinical reference, educational summary); Lipid screening and statin initiation guidance (searched, no relevant passage returned).*\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
